149-| Governance | Policies, approvals, evaluation sets | Declared constraints and the mechanisms that enforce or test them |
150-| Execution | Workflows, agent definitions, deployment bindings | The configuration selecting instructions, tools, and runtime conditions |
151:| Evidence | Manifests, run records, verification results | Retained observations and their links to artifacts and accepted work |
152-
153-I use <Term id="agentic-artifact-supply-chain">**agentic artifact supply chain**</Term> for the managed lifecycle of those reusable instructions, context, tools, policies and related assets. Existing work on agent supply chains and governed agent primitives already covers this ground; this is my organizing label for the lifecycle I'm building, not a new supply-chain analogy.[^primitives]
--
161-The lifecycle starts with **authoring**: the reviewer lives in one source repository, and a fix adds the missing guard. **Registration** makes that revision discoverable. **Approval** needs to attach to an exact revision and state its permitted use. The incident doesn't give me evidence of a complete approval record; it gives me a corrected source and a registry entry that didn't match deployment.
