/Users/miethe/dev/WIP/agents/theses/registry-wave/reg-wave-review-v4/registry_wave_experience_v2/tools/build_prototype.py-86-opening=section(0,'opening','The reviewer was supposed to check the work.', '<p class="lead">First I had to check the reviewer.</p><p class="prose">The source held the correction. The registry reflected it. Other projects used separate deployed copies, and those copies had not received the change.</p>'+scene(beats[0])+'''<p class="prose">The failure was not the absence of a registry. It was the unexamined handoff from a correct record to the copy another project used.</p><blockquote class="pull" data-component="PullQuote"><p>Copy and paste is fine for discovery,<br>but it will never be a lifecycle.</p></blockquote>''','THE INCIDENT')
/Users/miethe/dev/WIP/agents/theses/registry-wave/reg-wave-review-v4/registry_wave_experience_v2/tools/build_prototype.py-87-market=section(1,'registry-wave','What the registry wave solved','''<div class="prose"><p>A registry gives useful capabilities an identity, a place to be found, and a version to depend on. That is important infrastructure. It is not every assurance the consuming workflow needs.</p><p>This essay follows what happens after an artifact becomes available: which version a project intended to use, what was deployed, what a session loaded, and what the resulting work proved.</p></div><div class="table-wrap" data-component="EditorialTable"><table><caption>Four questions that should not be collapsed into one approval badge.</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Required distinction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Which capability?</td><td>Registered identity versus deployed and loaded identity.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted here?</td><td>Artifact approval versus conditions of this use.</td></tr><tr><td>What happened?</td><td>Configured behavior versus observed execution.</td></tr><tr><td>Did it meet the request?</td><td>Output versus verification and acceptance.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>''','THE PACKAGE')
/Users/miethe/dev/WIP/agents/theses/registry-wave/reg-wave-review-v4/registry_wave_experience_v2/tools/build_prototype.py:88:artifact=section(2,'artifacts','What counts as an artifact','''<div class="split" data-component="MediaSplit"><div><p class="lead">A reusable, versionable unit that shapes agent behavior.</p><p>Instructions, tools, context and constraints can all be dependencies of a working capability. Changing any one of them can change the behavior of the runtime that consumes it.</p><p>The runtime executes. A skill shapes its behavior. A signed context pack does not enforce the permissions it describes.</p><span class="pill violet">Definition / editorial framing</span></div><aside class="inset" data-component="InlineAside"><span class="glyph">◇</span><h3 style="margin-top:16px">A capability bundle</h3><div class="cap-stack"><span>Instructions and examples</span><span>Context and assumptions</span><span>Tool declarations and schemas</span><span>Constraints and evaluation criteria</span></div><span class="pill amber">Illustrative anatomy</span></aside></div><div class="table-wrap" id="taxonomy" data-component="EditorialTable"><table><caption>Artifact families retained from the supplied essay.</caption><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Examples</th><th>What needs review</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Instruction</td><td>Prompts, skills, commands, rules</td><td>Instructions and bundled executable dependencies</td></tr><tr><td>Tool</td><td>MCP servers, scripts, API adapters</td><td>Implementation, declarations and permission boundaries</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Context packs, memories, references</td><td>Provenance, freshness and scope</td></tr><tr><td>Governance</td><td>Policies, approvals, evaluations</td><td>Declared constraints and their enforcement or tests</td></tr><tr><td>Execution</td><td>Workflows, agent definitions, bindings</td><td>Configuration and runtime conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Manifests, run records, verification results</td><td>Observations and their links to work</td></tr></tbody></table></div>''','THE REUSABLE UNIT')
/Users/miethe/dev/WIP/agents/theses/registry-wave/reg-wave-review-v4/registry_wave_experience_v2/tools/build_prototype.py-89-follow=section(3,'follow-reviewer','Follow the reviewer out of the registry','<p class="lead">One name can conceal several different states.</p>'+''.join(scene(b) for b in beats[1:5]),'FOUR BOUNDARIES')
/Users/miethe/dev/WIP/agents/theses/registry-wave/reg-wave-review-v4/registry_wave_experience_v2/tools/build_prototype.py-90-variation=section(4,'variation','Some differences must survive','''<p class="lead">A different copy is not always a defective copy.</p><p class="prose">This is a separate, illustrative comparison—not the next historical step of the reviewer incident. Start with each project’s declared binding and then make the update decision.</p><span class="pill amber">Illustrative project states · not a security-waiver scenario</span><div class="variation" data-component="ManagedVariation"><div class="variant"><span class="eyebrow">PROJECT A</span><h3>Behind its binding</h3><p>The intended reference moved, but this deployed copy did not.</p><span class="decision">Review and update →</span></div><div class="variant"><span class="eyebrow">PROJECT B</span><h3>Useful local work</h3><p>A local change needs comparison before a shared update replaces it.</p><span class="decision">Reconcile; preserve justified work →</span></div><div class="variant"><span class="eyebrow">PROJECT C</span><h3>A deliberate pin</h3><p>The deployed copy matches an older declared experiment binding.</p><span class="decision">Retain only while policy permits →</span></div></div><p class="prose">No version, pin or local exception supplies its own approval. The value is an inspectable difference and an accountable decision about what happens next.</p>''','MANAGED VARIATION')
--
/Users/miethe/dev/WIP/agents/theses/registry-wave/reg-wave-review-v4/registry_wave_experience_v2/tools/build_prototype.py-96-sources=section(10,'sources','Sources and related work','''<p class="prose">The final page should render the reconciled research bibliography and public-safe source references. This design prototype does not replace those references with plausible-looking citations.</p><ul class="source-list"><li>Current manuscript and current image-usage handoff: content and asset authority.</li><li>Four research reports and the contribution-framing addendum: attribution authority, subject to their open items.</li><li>Primary lab captures: must be attached to the corresponding evidence records.</li></ul><p class="small muted">Publication dates, unresolved sources and runtime claims remain governed by the editorial handoff—not by these mockups.</p>''','SOURCE REGISTER')
